---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const images = `${base}images/remote-holder/`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Remote Holder">
  <header class="page-intro project-intro">
    <a href={`${base}work/`}>Back to work</a>
    <p class="eyebrow">Design study / Reverse engineering</p>
    <h1>Remote Holder</h1>
    <p class="intro muted">Three remotes of three different shapes, and a side table they were always loose on. Scanning both the remotes and the table gave me the geometry for a holder that fits all of it.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="project-opening" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
    <figure>
      <img src={`${images}remotes.jpg`} width="1400" height="1391" alt="Three remotes laid out on a gridded cutting mat, photographed from directly above: two long oval Govee remotes and one small rectangular six-button remote." fetchpriority="high" />
      <figcaption>The three remotes, squared up on a gridded mat.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="project-goal">
      <h2 id="problem-heading">Three shapes, one holder</h2>
      <p>The remotes have nothing in common. Two are long ovals with rounded ends, one is a small rounded rectangle. Anything generic enough to hold all three would hold none of them properly.</p>
      <p>That made it a fitment problem rather than a styling one, and fitment problems are answered by measuring the real thing rather than guessing at it.</p>
      <dl class="project-facts">
        <div><dt>Process</dt><dd>3D scanning, mesh cleanup, CAD, 3D printing</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scanner</dt><dd>Revopoint POP 4, turntable and global markers</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scanned</dt><dd>Each remote, and the side table it sits on</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Result</dt><dd>A single holder with a pocket per remote</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section text-step" aria-labelledby="capture-heading">
    <div class="process-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / Capture</p>
      <h2 id="capture-heading">Two scans doing two different jobs</h2>
      <p>I scanned each remote to get its outline and depth, and separately scanned the side table to get the surface the holder would sit on. The remotes define the pockets. The table defines the base.</p>
      <p>Scanning the environment rather than only the object is the part that made this different from the vacuum dock. A part can fit its contents perfectly and still sit badly where it lives.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section process-step" aria-labelledby="test-heading">
    <div class="process-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 / Test the fit first</p>
      <h2 id="test-heading">One pocket before three</h2>
      <p>Before committing to the full body I modelled a flat test coupon carrying a single pocket, so a wrong clearance would cost one small print instead of the whole holder.</p>
      <p>The coupon is 102 by 57 by 24 mm, which is enough material around the pocket to check the fit without printing anything that has to look like the final part.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src={`${images}fitment-test.jpg`} width="1400" height="1400" alt="Render of the fitment test coupon: a flat slab with a single shallow pocket cut into it." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The fitment coupon. Render of the model.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="section process-step" aria-labelledby="holder-heading">
    <div class="process-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 / The holder</p>
      <h2 id="holder-heading">A pocket shaped to each remote</h2>
      <p>The finished body is 140 by 97 by 62 mm and carries three pockets, each cut to the remote that goes in it: one rounded slot for the oval remotes and squarer pockets alongside. The face is angled so the remotes sit back rather than needing a lip to retain them.</p>
      <p>Photographs of the printed holder in place are still to come.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src={`${images}holder-cad.jpg`} width="1400" height="1400" alt="Render of the finished holder: an angled block with three separate pockets cut into its upper face." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The finished body. Render of the model.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <div class="project-end"><a href={`${base}work/`}>Back to all work</a></div>
</BaseLayout>
